.TH TERMTIME 1 2026-10-18 "termtime 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
termtime \- big block-letter clock for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termtime
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-1 ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B termtime
draws up to two lines of text in large block letters, centered on the
terminal. Each line has its own format, size and colors. Formats use the
same conversions as
.BR date (1),
so the lines can show the time, the date or any other text.
.PP
The screen is redrawn on every tick of the configured interval and
whenever the terminal is resized.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Use
.I file
instead of searching for a config.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Print the lines once to standard output and exit. This is also done
when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path
Print the path of the config in use and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Quit.
.TP
.B r
Reload the config. A config with errors is ignored and the previous one
stays in use.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reload the config, like
.BR r .
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM ", " SIGHUP
Restore the terminal and exit.
.TP
.B SIGTSTP
Restore the terminal and suspend; the clock is drawn again when resumed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config is a TOML file. The first one found is used:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR/termtime/config.toml
\fI$HOME\fR/.config/termtime/config.toml
/etc/termtime/config.toml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without any of them built-in defaults are used. Unknown options and
invalid values are reported as errors.
.SS [general]
.TP
.BR interval " (integer, default 1000)"
Redraw interval in milliseconds, 10 to 3600000. Redraws are aligned to
the wall clock, so 1000 ticks exactly on each second.
.TP
.BR spacing " (integer, default 1)"
Empty terminal rows between the two lines.
.TP
.BR fit " (boolean, default true)"
Shrink lines that do not fit the terminal, first to its width, then the
biggest line until both fit its height. Lines never get smaller than
scale 1.
.TP
.BR blocks " (string, default \(dqauto\(dq)"
How blocks are drawn:
.B \(dqunicode\(dq
uses the full block U+2588,
.B \(dqreverse\(dq
uses spaces in reverse video, which work with any font and character set,
and any single ASCII character such as
.B \(dq#\(dq
draws with that character.
.B \(dqauto\(dq
picks unicode on UTF-8 locales and reverse otherwise.
.TP
.BR colors " (\(dqauto\(dq, 256, 16 or 8, default \(dqauto\(dq)"
Colors the terminal supports. With 16 or 8 colors every configured color
is mapped to the nearest basic one. Auto detection uses 256 when
.B COLORTERM
is set or
.B TERM
names a 256 color terminal, 16 on the Linux console (bright colors are
drawn bold there), 8 for vt100-like or unset
.BR TERM ,
and 256 otherwise.
.TP
.BR locale " (string, default \(dqC\(dq)"
Locale for names such as
.B %A
and
.BR %B .
An empty string uses the locale from the environment. The font only has
ASCII glyphs, every other character is drawn as one placeholder glyph.
.SS [line1] and [line2]
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default true)"
Whether the line is shown. With one line disabled the other one is
centered alone.
.TP
.BR format " (string)"
Text to show, with
.BR date (1)
conversions. Defaults are
.B \(dq%H:%M:%S\(dq
and
.BR \(dq%Y\-%m\-%d\(dq .
.TP
.BR scale " (integer, 1 to 50)"
Size of the letters; a glyph is 7\(muscale columns by 5\(muscale rows.
Defaults are 2 and 1.
.TP
.BR color " (integer or string)"
Default color of the line: an ANSI 256 color number (0\-255) or one of
.BR black ", " red ", " green ", " yellow ", " blue ", " magenta ", "
.BR cyan ", " white ", " gray ,
.BR bright_black ", " bright_red ", " bright_green ", " bright_yellow ,
.BR bright_blue ", " bright_magenta ", " bright_cyan ", " bright_white .
.SS [line1.colors] and [line2.colors]
Colors for parts of the format. Each key is matched literally against
the format and the longest matching key wins, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[line1.colors]
"%H" = "bright_red"    # hours
":" = 8                # every colon
"%M:%S" = 10           # minutes, colon and seconds
.fi
.RE
.PP
A key for a plain conversion such as
.B \(dq%d\(dq
also colors its variants with flags or width such as
.BR %\-d .
Everything else uses the line's
.BR color .
.SH FORMAT
All conversions of
.BR strftime (3)
are supported, including the GNU flags
.BR \- " (no padding), " _ " (space padding), " 0 ", " ^ " (upper case) and "
.B #
and field widths. These
.BR date (1)
conversions are supported as well:
.TP
.B %N
Nanoseconds;
.B %3N
gives milliseconds,
.B %6N
microseconds. Use a shorter
.B interval
to see them move.
.TP
.B %q
Quarter of the year (1\-4).
.TP
.BR %:z ", " %::z ", " %:::z
Time zone offset as +hh:mm, +hh:mm:ss, or the shortest of +hh,
+hh:mm and +hh:mm:ss.
.TP
.B %%
A literal %.
.SH LINUX CONSOLE
On a virtual console
.RB ( TERM=linux )
termtime uses 16 colors and clears the screen on exit, as the console has
no alternate screen. Without a UTF-8 locale the blocks are drawn in
reverse video, which needs no special glyphs in the console font.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR TERM ", " COLORTERM
Used to detect the colors the terminal supports.
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE
A UTF-8 character set enables the block glyph.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Used to find the user config.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/termtime/config.toml
System wide config, documented with comments.
.SH EXAMPLES
A 12 hour clock with colored parts and the weekday below it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[line1]
format = "%I:%M:%S %p"
scale = 3
[line1.colors]
"%I" = "bright_red"
"%M" = "bright_green"
"%S" = "bright_blue"
":" = "gray"

[line2]
format = "%A"
color = 244
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1),
.BR strftime (3)
